   "content": "Follow these rules to determine if two product records refer to the same item:\n\n1. **Model Numbers:** Model numbers are the strongest matching signal. If both records have explicit model numbers, they must match exactly. A mismatch in model numbers indicates different products (e.g., different sizes, colors, or capacities), even if titles or prices are similar.\n2. **Brands:** Brand names must refer to the same manufacturer. Account for minor naming variations, parent companies, or legacy brand associations (e.g., Xerox and Tektronix) if model numbers and context match.\n3. **Product Variants:** Pay strict attention to capacity, size, color, and technical specifications mentioned in the titles or model numbers (e.g., 1 TB vs. 1.5 TB hard drives, black vs. copper mice). Different variants are not the same product.\n4. **Titles and Descriptions:** Minor wording differences, abbreviations, or missing descriptive terms in titles (e.g., \"778c hd color fishing system\" vs. \"778c hd fishfinder\") do not preclude a match if the core product and model numbers align.\n5. **Missing or Noisy Fields:** Missing values (such as a blank price) should be ignored. Minor category naming inconsistencies between retailers are acceptable as long as the core items match.",
