   "latency_s": 2.471,
   "content": "Follow these rules to determine if two product records refer to the same item:\n\n1. **Model Numbers:** The model number is the strongest indicator. If model numbers are present in both records, they must match exactly. A mismatch in model numbers indicates different products, even if titles are otherwise similar.\n2. **Brands:** Brands must be consistent. Treat obvious parent-subsidiary or synonymous brand variants (e.g., Xerox and Tektronix) as a match if the model numbers match and context supports it.\n3. **Variants (Size, Color, Capacity, Part Numbers):** Core specifications such as storage capacity (e.g., 1 TB vs. 1.5 TB), dimensions, color, or specific part suffixes (e.g., MP2800BLK vs. MP2375BLK) dictate distinct products. Any variance in these attributes means they are not the same product.\n4. **Titles and Categories:** Category names and title phrasing may vary across retailers. Minor title differences, missing generic descriptors, or abbreviated words do not disqualify a match as long as the core item and model number align.\n5. **Bundles and Accessories:** Pay attention to secondary items or specific model variants embedded in the title. If one record specifies a distinct kit, case, or generation, verify that the underlying model numbers match.\n6. **Missing Fields:** Missing fields (such as a blank price or omitted category) should be ignored; do not penalize a pair for lacking non-essential data.",
