  "qwen2.5:72b#0": {
   "model": "qwen2.5:72b",
   "provider": "purdue",
   "status": 200,
   "latency_s": 19.538,
   "content": "### Matching Rules:\n\n1. **Brand and Model Number Match**: \n   - If the brand and model number of both records match, consider them as the same product.\n   \n2. **Product Title Variants**:\n   - Allow for minor differences in product titles (e.g., \"fishing system\" vs. \"fishfinder\") if other key fields match.\n   - Ignore additional details like \"usb\" or \"with buffalo tools\" that do not change the core product.\n\n3. **Product Category**:\n   - Differences in category names are acceptable as long as the brand, model number, and title are consistent.\n\n4. **Color and Size Variants**:\n   - If the model numbers differ due to color (e.g., \"blk\" vs. \"copper\"), treat them as different products.\n   - Similarly, if the capacity or size differs (e.g., 1.5 TB vs. 1 TB), consider them as different products.\n\n5. **Bundles and Accessories**:\n   - If both records refer to a similar product but one is a bundle or includes accessories (e.g., \"maintenance kit\" with different brands), treat them as the same product if the model number matches.\n   \n6. **Missing or Noisy Fields**:\n   - Missing price information should not affect the match decision if other key fields align.\n   - Ignore minor discrepancies in title or category that do not impact the core identity of the product.\n\n7. **Model Number Variations**:\n   - If the model numbers are very similar but slightly different (e.g., \"twin2x4096-6400c5c\" vs. \"twin2x2048-6400\"), treat them as different products unless other fields strongly suggest otherwise.\n\nBy following these rules, the assistant can accurately determine whether two product records refer to the same real-world product.",
