  "qwen2.5:72b#1": {
   "model": "qwen2.5:72b",
   "provider": "purdue",
   "status": 200,
   "latency_s": 14.061,
   "content": "### Matching Rules for Product Records:\n\n1. **Brand and Model Number**:\n   - If the brand and model number match exactly, consider it a strong indicator of the same product.\n   - If only one record has a model number or if they differ slightly (e.g., different suffixes), check other fields for consistency.\n\n2. **Product Title**:\n   - Compare the core elements of the title (product name, key features).\n   - Ignore minor variations in wording (e.g., \"fishing system\" vs. \"fishfinder\").\n   - If the titles are significantly different but the model number and brand match, consider them the same product unless other fields strongly suggest otherwise.\n\n3. **Product Category**:\n   - Minor differences in category (e.g., \"fishing and boating\" vs. \"fishfinders\") can be ignored if other criteria match.\n   - Significant category differences (e.g., \"memory\" vs. \"computer components\") should raise a flag but are not conclusive on their own.\n\n4. **Product Variants**:\n   - If the product variants (color, size, capacity) differ, they are likely different products (e.g., black mouse vs. copper mouse).\n   - However, if the core product is the same and only minor accessories or features differ (e.g., \"3 switchback\" vs. \"hybrid case for iPod touch 4G\"), consider them the same product.\n\n5. **Bundles and Accessories**:\n   - If one record describes a bundle or an accessory that includes the main product, they can still be considered the same if the core product (brand, model number) matches.\n   - If the records describe different accessories or bundles for the same core product, consider them the same product unless there is a significant price difference.\n\n6. **Missing or Noisy Fields**:\n   - Missing fields should not automatically disqualify a match; focus on available key information (brand, model number, title).\n   - If one record has noisy data (e.g., extra descriptions), compare the essential elements to determine if they align with the other record.\n\n7. **Price**:\n   - Price differences alone are not sufficient to conclude that products are different, especially if all other criteria match.\n   - Significant price discrepancies should be reviewed in context with other fields to ensure accuracy.\n\nBy applying these rules, you can accurately determine whether two product records refer to the same real-world product.",
